1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,892
A volunteer spends an hour mentoring a

2
00:00:01,950 --> 00:00:02,577
child.

3
00:00:03,042 --> 00:00:05,283
Before she leaves, she learns the family

4
00:00:05,341 --> 00:00:07,964
needs diapers tonight, so she posts a
brief

5
00:00:08,022 --> 00:00:08,615
update.

6
00:00:09,358 --> 00:00:11,331
By morning it is buried under dozens of

7
00:00:11,389 --> 00:00:12,156
messages.

8
00:00:12,562 --> 00:00:14,222
The hour may never be counted.

9
00:00:14,768 --> 00:00:16,625
The family may still be waiting.

10
00:00:17,233 --> 00:00:19,532
This is the problem behind challenge five:

11
00:00:19,880 --> 00:00:22,307
coordinators rebuild their week from
chats,

12
00:00:22,411 --> 00:00:26,138
texts and photos — forty volunteers, four

13
00:00:26,184 --> 00:00:27,937
hours a week, by hand.

14
00:00:28,680 --> 00:00:30,817
Volunteer Relay turns those updates into

15
00:00:30,875 --> 00:00:33,615
traceable records, and needs someone owns.

16
00:00:34,358 --> 00:00:36,889
Here is the coordinator's inbox, with
review

17
00:00:36,935 --> 00:00:37,852
work waiting.

18
00:00:38,467 --> 00:00:40,626
Volunteers report in their own words.

19
00:00:41,369 --> 00:00:43,401
This update describes one shared garden

20
00:00:43,436 --> 00:00:46,234
visit, two volunteers, and the time each
of

21
00:00:46,281 --> 00:00:47,012
them gave.

22
00:00:47,755 --> 00:00:49,775
Relay proposes an activity and two

23
00:00:49,833 --> 00:00:52,271
contributions, and keeps every proposed

24
00:00:52,329 --> 00:00:54,245
field tied to the original message.

25
00:00:54,988 --> 00:00:57,647
Nothing is approved until staff decide.

26
00:00:58,267 --> 00:01:00,461
One message holds a visit for two people.

27
00:01:01,204 --> 00:01:03,364
One shared hour means one visit and two

28
00:01:03,422 --> 00:01:06,220
volunteer-hours, so approving it records
two

29
00:01:06,324 --> 00:01:08,310
separate contributions of sixty minutes

30
00:01:08,356 --> 00:01:08,786
each.

31
00:01:09,656 --> 00:01:11,827
Staff can inspect the evidence first, and

32
00:01:11,909 --> 00:01:14,869
anything missing or unresolved stays
visible

33
00:01:14,985 --> 00:01:16,134
instead of being guessed.

34
00:01:16,355 --> 00:01:17,446
Nothing is guessed.

35
00:01:18,067 --> 00:01:20,447
A later update describes the same visit.

36
00:01:21,190 --> 00:01:23,315
Counting every message would inflate the

37
00:01:23,361 --> 00:01:25,834
totals, so Relay raises the possible

38
00:01:25,869 --> 00:01:27,343
duplicate for staff review.

39
00:01:28,353 --> 00:01:30,501
Rejecting it leaves the approved ledger

40
00:01:30,606 --> 00:01:32,893
unchanged, and the decision stays attached

41
00:01:32,928 --> 00:01:33,903
to its source.

42
00:01:34,533 --> 00:01:36,739
An urgent need should never wait on a
buried

43
00:01:36,774 --> 00:01:37,424
message.

44
00:01:37,970 --> 00:01:40,675
Staff assign an owner, set an
acknowledgment

45
00:01:40,733 --> 00:01:43,693
deadline, and later record the outcome.

46
00:01:44,436 --> 00:01:46,654
If acknowledgment stalls, the worker

47
00:01:46,735 --> 00:01:48,175
escalates at the deadline.

48
00:01:49,359 --> 00:01:52,029
Delivery is not acknowledgment, and the

49
00:01:52,087 --> 00:01:54,130
board shows what still needs attention.

50
00:01:55,001 --> 00:01:57,381
Acknowledgment is a person, not a delivery

51
00:01:57,439 --> 00:01:58,171
receipt.

52
00:01:58,800 --> 00:02:01,180
At the end of the week the report comes
from

53
00:02:01,226 --> 00:02:03,142
the same records the team used to do its

54
00:02:03,177 --> 00:02:03,618
work.

55
00:02:04,257 --> 00:02:07,496
Approved hours, contributions, open needs

56
00:02:07,542 --> 00:02:09,957
and pending items are counted
consistently,

57
00:02:10,422 --> 00:02:12,767
and unknown time stays unknown rather than

58
00:02:12,813 --> 00:02:13,835
becoming zero.

59
00:02:14,706 --> 00:02:16,795
Every decision is kept in an audit history

60
00:02:16,830 --> 00:02:19,268
with its source, so report and work share

61
00:02:19,326 --> 00:02:20,162
one source.

62
00:02:20,767 --> 00:02:21,882
Volunteer Relay.

63
00:02:22,520 --> 00:02:25,713
Every hour counted, every need connected.

64
00:02:26,351 --> 00:02:28,673
Built for nonprofit teams, with fictional

65
00:02:28,743 --> 00:02:31,680
demo data and an open-source
implementation.

66
00:02:32,551 --> 00:02:35,407
The repository holds the code, the plan
and

67
00:02:35,488 --> 00:02:36,696
the recording notes.
